hypotheses.statement:
For $\lambda\neq1$ and every $q=0,1,2$, $$\label{surface:acyclic}
 H^q(X,P_\lambda)=0,
 \qquad H^q(X,K_X\otimes P_\lambda)=0.$$ For every $\lambda\in\mathbb C^*$, including $\lambda=1$, $$\label{surface:canonical-vanishing}
 H^0(X,K_X\otimes P_\lambda)=0.$$ At the trivial character, $H^0(X,\mathcal O_X)=\mathbb C$, $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)=\mathbb C$, and $H^2(X,\mathcal O_X)=0$.

For $p\in\{0,2\}$ and $0\leq q\leq2$, the integration pairing gives a perfect pairing $$\label{weighted:serre}
 H^{p,q}_{a,b}(Y,E)\ \times\
 H^{2-p,2-q}_{-a,-b}(Y,E^{-1})\longrightarrow\mathbb C.$$ Here both pairs of rates are admissible.

If $a\in L^1(\mathcal C)\cap L^2(\mathcal C)$, there is a periodic distribution $g$ satisfying $$\tfrac12(\partial_t+i\partial_\theta)g=a,
 \qquad
 \sup_{j\in\mathbb Z}\|g\|_{W^{1,2}(S_j)}
       \leq C\bigl(\|a\|_{L^1}+\|a\|_{L^2}\bigr).$$ For every $p<\infty$ this solution satisfies $$\label{cylinder:gauge-exp}
 \sup_j\int_{S_j}\exp\bigl(p|\operatorname{Re}g|\bigr)dA_v<\infty.$$ If $a$ is smooth in an open set, $g$ is smooth there.

outputs.exact_statement:
Put $b=b_2(X)$. There is a basis $u_1,\ldots,u_b$ of $H^2(X;\mathbb Z)/\operatorname{Tors}$ with $$\label{surface:canonical-basis}
 u_i\cdot u_j=-\delta_{ij},\qquad
 K_X=u_1+\cdots+u_b\pmod{\operatorname{Tors}}.$$ For every curve $B\subset X$, $$\label{surface:curve-degrees}
 B^2<0,\qquad (K_X+B)\cdot B\leq0,\qquad K_X\cdot B\geq0.$$ Consequently every effective divisor has nonnegative canonical degree.

For $\lambda\neq1$ and every $q=0,1,2$, $$\label{surface:acyclic}
 H^q(X,P_\lambda)=0,
 \qquad H^q(X,K_X\otimes P_\lambda)=0.$$ For every $\lambda\in\mathbb C^*$, including $\lambda=1$, $$\label{surface:canonical-vanishing}
 H^0(X,K_X\otimes P_\lambda)=0.$$ At the trivial character, $H^0(X,\mathcal O_X)=\mathbb C$, $H^1(X,\mathcal O_X)=\mathbb C$, and $H^2(X,\mathcal O_X)=0$. If a holomorphic function $f$ on $Y$ satisfies $f\circ T-f=c$ for a constant $c$, then $f$ is constant and $c=0$. More generally, a holomorphic function annihilated by a positive power of $T^*-1$ is constant.

There is a constant $C_0\geq0$, independent of $n$, such that for each $n$ one can choose smooth line-bundle data $E_1,\ldots,E_d$ on $Y$, supported in the interior of $W_n$, with $$\label{compact:rank-bound}
 d\geq n b_2(X)-C_0.$$ They are obtained by cutting line bundles on $X_n$ that are trivialized near $Z$. Their transferred Chern classes $w_1,\ldots,w_d\in H^2(X_n;\mathbb Z)$ are linearly independent over $\mathbb Q$ modulo torsion. Their compact Chern classes $e_i$ satisfy $$\label{compact:numerical-data}
 e_i^2=K_Y\cdot e_i=-m_i<0$$ for positive integers $m_i$. Pairings of these compact classes equal the corresponding pairings of the transferred classes on $X_n$.

For $p\in\{0,2\}$ and $0\leq q\leq2$, the integration pairing gives a perfect pairing $$\label{weighted:serre}
 H^{p,q}_{a,b}(Y,E)\ \times\
 H^{2-p,2-q}_{-a,-b}(Y,E^{-1})\longrightarrow\mathbb C.$$ Here both pairs of rates are admissible. The statement concerns the cohomology classes in any of the stepped realizations above; the pairing is evaluated on their smooth weighted harmonic representatives.

Let $E$ be a compact datum supported in a cut-open period of a finite cyclic quotient $X_n=Y/T^n$, with its trivializations fixed in collars of the cut. Equip $E$ with an integrable operator $d_E$ satisfying <a href="#weighted:end-error" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="weighted:end-error">[weighted:end-error]</a>, and let $e$ be its compact support Chern class. For $a,b>0$ its weighted Dolbeault index is $$\label{weighted:index-formula}
 \sum_{q=0}^2(-1)^q\dim H^{0,q}_{a,b}(Y,E)
 =1+\frac12\bigl(e^2-K_Y\cdot e\bigr).$$ The products on the right are compact support intersection pairings. In particular, if $e^2=K_Y\cdot e$, the section space is one-dimensional, its positive-end coefficient is nonzero on every nonzero section, and $H^{0,1}_{a,b}(Y,E)=0$.

Let $E$ be one of the data in Lemma <a href="#compact:classes" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="compact:classes">9</a>, and write $e^2=K_Y\cdot e=-m$ with $m>0$. Given any finite $\gamma>0$, $E$ admits a holomorphic structure whose Dolbeault operator, in its fixed end frames, differs from the trivial operator by $O(e^{-\gamma|h|})$ in every derivative. For this structure there is a holomorphic section $s$ such that, in those frames, $$\label{compact:end-limits}
 s\longrightarrow1\quad(h\to+\infty),\qquad
 s\longrightarrow0\quad(h\to-\infty),$$ with exponentially decaying errors. Its effective divisor $D=(s=0)$ is contained in $\{h\leq H\}$ for some $H$, is locally finite, and represents the ordinary Chern class $j(e)$, or equivalently its dual locally finite homology class.

There are an integer $P\geq2$ and positive constants $Q,\delta,\varepsilon_0,D_0$, depending only on the lifted geometry, with the following property. Suppose that $\Omega\subset\mathcal C$ is a domain and $f:\Omega\to Y$ is a nonconstant proper holomorphic map. A strip $S_j$ is called *flat* if $\overline{C_j^{2P+2}}\subset\Omega$ and the image of this entire thickened cylinder under $f$ is contained in $U_p(r)$ for one of the fixed coordinate charts. Every flat strip satisfies $\sup_{S_j}u\leq D_0$.*


*For every interval $I\subset\mathbb R$, if $S_j$ contains a point $v_j$ with $h(f(v_j))\in I$ and $S_j$ is not flat, then $$\label{cylinder:energy-cost}
 \int_{\Omega\cap C_j^Q\cap(h\circ f)^{-1}(I+[-\delta,\delta])}
              u^2\,dA_v\geq\varepsilon_0.$$ The same assertion holds on a source half-cylinder whenever $\overline{C_j^Q}$ lies in its interior.

There are constants $R_0,C_0>0$ depending only on the lifted geometry with the following property. Let $f:\Omega\to Y$ be a nonconstant proper holomorphic map from a domain in $\mathcal C$. Let $w\geq0$ be measurable and suppose $$M_w:=\sup_{j\in\mathbb Z}\|w\|_{L^2(\Omega\cap S_j)}<\infty.$$ For every interval $I$ of length one, set $$E_I=\int_{\Omega\cap(h\circ f)^{-1}(I+[-R_0,R_0])}
                          u^2\,dA_v.$$ If $E_I<\infty$, then $$\label{cylinder:estimate-formula}
 \int_{\Omega\cap(h\circ f)^{-1}(I)}u\,w\,dA_v
            \leq C_0M_w(1+E_I).$$ The same estimate holds on a full source half-cylinder cut from a proper normalization map; properness is used only on thickened strips away from the artificial initial boundary, and a fixed finite initial block of strips is included in the constant. The integral on the left is always restricted to the inverse image of the target interval $I$.

If $a\in L^1(\mathcal C)\cap L^2(\mathcal C)$, there is a periodic distribution $g$ satisfying $$\tfrac12(\partial_t+i\partial_\theta)g=a,
 \qquad
 \sup_{j\in\mathbb Z}\|g\|_{W^{1,2}(S_j)}
       \leq C\bigl(\|a\|_{L^1}+\|a\|_{L^2}\bigr).$$ For every $p<\infty$ this solution satisfies $$\label{cylinder:gauge-exp}
 \sup_j\int_{S_j}\exp\bigl(p|\operatorname{Re}g|\bigr)dA_v<\infty.$$ If $a$ is smooth in an open set, $g$ is smooth there.
